# Space CODEOWNERS (§4.2, D-011): gated paths ONLY.
#
# /<system>/provides/** entries do NOT belong here at template time — each
# system's onboarding PR adds its own entry when it joins (US-102, §9.2
# step 2). Pre-seeding a system entry here would be a layout invention this
# template does not make.
#
# ── AN OWNER THAT DOES NOT EXIST FAILS SILENTLY ───────────────────────────
#
# Replace BOTH halves of every placeholder below: the org AND the name after
# the slash. `@REPLACE_WITH_ORG/space-admins` is a TEAM reference, and the
# team `space-admins` almost certainly does not exist in your org — nothing
# creates it. GitHub does not reject an unknown owner; it ignores the line.
# So the natural literal edit (swap the org, keep the team name) produces a
# file that LOOKS like it gates `/space.yaml` and gates nothing.
#
# That matters more than it sounds: the code-owner review requirement is the
# whole of the G4 safety argument (BRANCH-PROTECTION.md), and this is its
# only mechanism. An inert CODEOWNERS is an ungated trust root, with no
# error anywhere to tell you.
#
# Verified on a real org 2026-07-26: the placeholder's team names did not
# exist there, while three unrelated teams did — exactly the state in which a
# copied placeholder is silently inert.
#
# Individual logins are the safe default and are what a small space wants:
#
#   /space.yaml     @alice
#   /decisions/**   @alice @bob
#
# Use a team only if you have confirmed it exists and that its members are
# who you mean. After editing, check GitHub's own CODEOWNERS view (Settings
# → Branches, or the file's page) — it flags unknown owners there, and that
# flag is the only feedback you will get.

/space.yaml     @REPLACE_WITH_ORG/REPLACE_WITH_TEAM_OR_LOGIN
/decisions/**   @REPLACE_WITH_ORG/REPLACE_WITH_TEAM_OR_LOGIN
